\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Motivation and aim}
\date{2026-11-11}


\p{The continuum is widely used to model physical reality. We take it to have no
physical counterpart at quantum-spacetime scales: lengths and times below the
Planck length and the Planck time lie beyond measurement
\citet{Section 3.1}{hossenfelder2013minimal}, though a minimal length
does not by itself force discreteness
\citet{Section 5}{hossenfelder2013minimal}. Continuum models also produce
divergences and singularities, which may reflect ignorance of physics at
very high energies \citet{Section 2}{carlip2001quantum}, and Wilson argues
that every continuous model suffers both an ultraviolet catastrophe and an
infrared catastrophe
\citet{reply of 12 April 2022, 8:56 am}{wilson2022mathematicism}. Attempts
to quantize gravity have brought the
difficulty into focus, since those singularities and the infinities of
perturbation theory are among the problems they hope to resolve.}

\p{Lattice field theory, by contrast, keeps the continuum theory as its object
and uses the lattice to compute and to regulate. The spacing is a
short-distance cutoff, removed in the continuum limit as that spacing goes to
zero \citet{Section 7}{wingate2025lattice},
\citet{Section 3.7}{hossenfelder2013minimal}.}

\p{We take the proper mathematical tools for these challenges to be a theory that is
fundamentally discrete, with the finite structure as the object and the
continuum an emergent, large-scale description. The notes therefore build the
mathematics for such a physics from finite structures, by exact algebra and
with no limits inside those finite constructions, and ask whether geometry,
time and matter can be induced from them, revealing new physics insight near
the Planck scale while agreeing with continuum physics at large scales.}

\p{On a finite structure, what continuum analysis does by limits can be done only
by algebra, and only with the structure present at each stage, which may lie
below geometry and topology. The notes therefore look for an algebraic form of
calculus that keeps those of its laws that survive, above all the theorems
relating differentiation to integration, and treat a law that fails as a
result. At each finite size no divergence arises, so the catastrophes above
become a question about the limit rather than about the constructions.
Agreement with continuum physics then concerns how finite structures relate: a
law is recovered when it holds on each structure and is preserved by
refinement and coarse-graining, and the continuum is a limit of such a system,
shrinking a mesh being one instance.}

\p{What a theory builds in should not be postulated, but emerge as a consequence
of the underlying mathematics. Physics usually works the other way: quantum
theory is written over the complex numbers by default, with operators computed
as matrices, a representation that depends on a basis, makes distinct
structures look alike and hides the choices it makes, so distinctions the
physics needs are restored by added conditions. The notes instead let each
problem determine the algebra to work in, so that such distinctions are kept
from the start.}

\p{An equally important question concerns the parameters of a model: which
should be determined and which left free, and what principle governs the
division? Cosmological bubble-universe pictures describe regions that may
settle into different vacuum states, with different effective low-energy
physics \citet{§§2–3}{guth2007eternal}. Wilson separately proposes that
particle masses depend on the surrounding gravitational environment
\citek{wilson2020vacua}. We draw inspiration from these pictures and extend
them into a mental model for the mathematics developed here, without adopting
a particular mechanism for forming a bubble.}

\p{In our picture, a bubble universe must have symmetries that allow it to
persist and develop rich physical structure. A particular bubble must “pick”
one such symmetry structure. Ideally, most fundamental parameters would follow
from the chosen symmetries. Among those left free by symmetry, some would be
fixed by the bubble’s initial conditions. Others would depend on the local
background, so describing a concrete physical process would require specifying
its surrounding environment.}

\p{Background independence is usually counted a desirable feature of a fundamental
theory, and Wilson argues that it is a hidden assumption rather than a property the
Standard Model possesses \citek{wilson2025background}. Here the background is the
local environment. Mach's principle says that inertia is determined by the
distribution of matter. The further claim, that fundamental parameters may depend
on that environment, is Wilson's reading of the principle, and the notes keep
their constructions compatible with a physics in which a parameter may so depend.}

\p{The same principle applies to spacetime. General relativity takes spacetime to
be a curved manifold of fixed dimension and signature whose metric is the
gravitational field, and takes the equality of inertial and gravitational mass
as a founding principle, so it does not explain why either holds. Here we see
quantities like the metric and mass as data of how fields couple to a fixed
finite structure: a field's inertial mass belongs to its own operator, its
gravitational mass to its coupling, and whether they are equal becomes a
property of that coupling. Curvature then need not be assumed: what it
describes is sought in the coupling itself.}

\p{Physics motivates the notes, but the interest and the focus are the mathematics
that answers the challenges: no physical theory is claimed, and where the notes
include a falsifiable proposal for one, it is an example of applying the tools
developed here. The organizing
hypothesis is that operations which continuum analysis presents analytically can
be recast as algebra on finite-dimensional objects; the notes test it on small
cases before relying on it, and a proposed translation carries its carrier, its
map and the property proved of it.}
